+# EffectCraft ChatGPT connection
+
+This local modification adds an optional native ChatGPT connection to the Windows desktop app.
+Other platforms retain their original editor and explicitly report unsupported auth commands.
+Open **ChatGPT connection…** at the bottom right, then **Continue with ChatGPT**.
+The browser asks you to authorize this app independently of the other Craft apps.
+Account connection is implemented; AI editing and inference are not part of this change.
+
+## Protocol and storage
+
+- Official dynamic registration, a stable app-specific UUID host, loopback callback,
+  PKCE S256, state/nonce checks, and verified RS256/ES256 OpenAI ID tokens.
+- Issued client IDs are retained before code exchange. Reauthorization is bound to
+  the selected client and verified identity. Disconnect removes the registration.
+- Granted scopes determine plan permission. Renewal atomically replaces rotating tokens.
+- Tokens are encrypted with AES-256-GCM; the encryption key lives in the Windows
+  Credential Manager. No plaintext storage fallback. Windows Schannel provides TLS, avoiding a new C/C++ crypto or D-Bus build dependency. Separate app directories.
+- One process owns credential rotation at a time. Callback and HTTPS reads are bounded.
+- Disconnect clears local credentials and attempts remote revocation. If that fails,
+  the app directs you to https://chatgpt.com/settings/usage.
+- The desktop wrapper forwards all editor lifecycle hooks, preferences, autosaves,
+  and native-menu input. Authentication remains native desktop-only; engine/document formats are unchanged.
+- Authentication commands are native platform operations, available headlessly:
+  `effectcraft --chatgpt-status`, `--chatgpt-sign-in`, `--chatgpt-refresh`, and
+  `--chatgpt-disconnect`. Status never returns credentials.
+
+## Validation status (2026-10-10)
+
+Run 37954342001 passed all 174 GPU tests with no failures, ignores or filters,
+including the scalar-blur regression and the original environment, RGB, Bend It
+and Page Turn comparisons. The entire GPU suite passed again during workspace
+testing. All 390 engine tests, workspace formatting and strict workspace Clippy
+passed. All eight added authentication tests also passed in workspace testing.
+
+Full CI stopped at `tests_auto::auto_backend_keeps_fast_accelerators`: 176 render
+tests passed, one failed and one existing test was ignored. The mock accelerator
+was asked for only 3 of 40 frames against the unchanged requirement of >=36.
+The zero-delay mock still called `std::thread::sleep(Duration::ZERO)`. On Windows
+Rust invokes Sleep(0), which yields the thread time slice; on Unix it returns
+immediately. That scheduling cost is a likely cause of the unexpected CPU choice.
+
+The fixture now skips sleeping for zero simulated delay and includes timing
+statistics in the original assertion. The production AutoPick code and >=36
+criterion are unchanged. The next recipe runs every renderer test early, then
+the complete GPU suite and upstream CI. Workspace --no-fail-fast reports all
+failed targets while keeping a failure status. Formatting, strict lint, layers,
+assets, wasm and authentication/UI gates remain required.
+
+Revised source is prepared. A successful replacement full run and its explicit
+connection-panel preview are still required. Actual account authorization remains
+untested; no installed or signed application release was created. Local Cargo
+remains blocked by Windows Application Control; no security setting changed.
+
+Evidence: https://github.com/SkeltionCR/craft-chatgpt-validation/actions/runs/37954342001
+Rust sleep behavior: https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/thread/fn.sleep.html
+Windows Sleep behavior: https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/api/synchapi/nf-synchapi-sleep
+
+On an authorized build machine, run:
+
+```sh
+cargo fmt --all
+cargo test -p effectcraft chatgpt
+cargo clippy -p effectcraft --all-targets -- -D warnings
+cargo xtask ci
+cargo run -p effectcraft
+```
+
+Tests cover the RFC 7636 PKCE vector, hostile callbacks, returning client binding,
+scope/expiry checks, token-redacted status, signed identity claims, forged ID tokens, and encrypted-data corruption.
+The control test exercises sign-in dispatch and cancellation without reading credentials.
+An explicitly requested offscreen test renders the actual connection panel on a test editor surface.
+Then test a real sign-in, restart, renewal, cancellation, disconnect, and editor save/quit.
+
+Source contract: https://developers.openai.com/siwc/token-sharing-open-source/sign-in
+UI guidance: https://developers.openai.com/siwc/ui-ux-guidelines
